[0.007, "o", "\r\n\u001b[1;35m━━━ 3. Search: original passages only, no generated answer ━━━\u001b[0m\r\n"]
[1.005, "o", "\u001b[1m$ ./wiki search price discrimination -k 3\u001b[0m\r\n"]
[1.120, "o", "\u001b[1mSearch\u001b[0m (hybrid (bm25 + embeddings, RRF)): price discrimination\r\n\r\n\u001b[36m[P1] raw/micro-final-cheat-sheet.md › Price Discrimination\u001b[0m  \u001b[2mcos 0.63 · bm25 8.1\u001b[0m\r\n"]
[0.000, "o", "  Quantity discount (menu pricing, but for different quantity)\r\n  (1) price for LQ equal to low WTP, (2) price for HQ that makes high type indifferent, (3) Check if\r\n  price is higher if selling only high or low quantity\r\n  When more units, P has to incorporate CS to the first unit\r\n  Condition: seller wants to sell larger Q to the high type\r\n\r\n\u001b[36m[P2] raw/micro-final-cheat-sheet.md › Price Discrimination\u001b[0m  \u001b[2mcos 0.58 · bm25 7.1\u001b[0m\r\n"]
[0.000, "o", "  PRICE DISCRIMINATION\r\n  Charge different prices not due to cost differences\r\n  Goal: capture consumer surplus and eliminate deadweight loss\r\n  First-degree (perfect): Each consumer charged WTP. Rare in practice, requires perfect information\r\n  Third-degree (segment/group): Groups face different prices (ex: students, seniors, geography,\r\n  business).\r\n  Conditions: 1) Different buyers have different WTP; 2) Ability to identify who has high/low WTP;\r\n  3) Ability to segment buyers with different WTP.\r\n  Restrictions: Arbitrage, legal\r\n  Second-degree (menu): offering different versions of a product to introduce lower/higher value\r\n  buyers to reveal themselves\r\n  Downside: Not perfect (CS and DWL - has to give value to consum…\r\n\r\n\u001b[36m[P3] raw/micro-examples-in-the-wild-3.md › Examples in the Wild 3\u001b[0m  \u001b[2mcos 0.57 · bm25 6.8\u001b[0m\r\n"]
[0.001, "o", "  When I joined Egon Zehnder (EZ), their services had a single price across all clients. We soon\r\n  realized that smaller companies, like startups, could not afford the full fee asked, while listed\r\n  clients were less sensitive to price changes. To address this, we introduced third-degree pricing\r\n  discrimination based on company size and segment. Startups and digital firms paid lower fees,\r\n  while large listed companies paid more. This allowed EZ to win projects with elastic clients,\r\n  while capturing additional revenue from less price-sensitive ones.\r\n\r\n\u001b[2msaved runs/search/20260922-182125-price-discrimination.md\u001b[0m\r\n"]
[0.042, "o", "\r\n\u001b[1;35m━━━ 4. Ask-mode evals (standalone, cited) ━━━\u001b[0m\r\n"]
[1.008, "o", "\u001b[1m$ ./wiki ask What are the eight behaviors of a great manager identified by Google's Project Oxygen?\u001b[0m\r\n"]
[11.045, "o", "\u001b[1mAsk\u001b[0m · mlx-community/gemma-4-e4b-it-4bit · local\r\n\r\n"]
[0.000, "o", "  The eight behaviors of a great manager, according to Google’s Project Oxygen, are:\r\n  1. Good coach [S1].\r\n  2. Empowers (does not micromanage) [S1].\r\n  3. Cares about people [S1].\r\n  4. Is results-oriented [S1].\r\n  5. Is a good communicator [S1].\r\n  6. Supports careers [S1].\r\n  7. Has a clear vision/strategy [S1].\r\n  8. Possesses technical expertise [S1].\r\n\r\n\u001b[1mCitations\u001b[0m (answered_with_citations)\r\n  [S1] raw/leading-people-midterm-study-guide.md › Managing People\r\n\r\n\u001b[2m6.38 s · 34.6 tok/s · MLX peak 5.86 GB · saved runs/ask/20260922-182137-what-are-the-eight-behaviors-of-a-great-manager-id.md\u001b[0m"]
[0.000, "o", "\r\n"]
[0.152, "o", "\u001b[1m$ ./wiki ask What cheaper service did my former executive search firm create so that startups could afford to work with us?\u001b[0m\r\n"]
[9.672, "o", "\u001b[1mAsk\u001b[0m · mlx-community/gemma-4-e4b-it-4bit · local\r\n\r\n  The firm introduced a lower-priced version of its service to accommodate startups, which were\r\n  considered \"low type\" clients [S1]. This was part of a strategy involving third-degree pricing\r\n  discrimination based on company size and segment [S2]. This allowed the firm to expand its\r\n  customer base and increase revenue by offering a version that startups could afford [S1].\r\n\r\n\u001b[1mCitations\u001b[0m (answered_with_citations)\r\n"]
[0.000, "o", "  [S1] raw/micro-examples-in-the-wild-4.md › Examples in the Wild 4\r\n  [S2] raw/micro-examples-in-the-wild-3.md › Examples in the Wild 3\r\n\r\n\u001b[2m4.78 s · 34.9 tok/s · MLX peak 5.85 GB · saved runs/ask/20260922-182147-what-cheaper-service-did-my-former-executive-searc.md\u001b[0m\r\n"]
[0.140, "o", "\u001b[1m$ ./wiki ask What is a sunk cost, and how did I fall into the sunk cost trap at a restaurant?\u001b[0m\r\n"]
[11.929, "o", "\u001b[1mAsk\u001b[0m · mlx-community/gemma-4-e4b-it-4bit · local\r\n\r\n"]
[0.001, "o", "  A sunk cost is a cost that cannot be recovered and is irrelevant for future decisions [S3]. Sunk\r\n  cost mistakes occur when future actions are taken to justify past choices, even though those past\r\n  costs are unchangeable and irrelevant for forward thinking [S1].\r\n\r\n  In the restaurant example, you had paid for a large pizza, and after eating three slices, you\r\n  realized you were full [S4]. You felt compelled to finish the pizza because you had already paid\r\n  for it, which is an example of falling into the sunk cost trap [S1, S4]. The mistake could have\r\n  been avoided by ordering a smaller pizza or by focusing on future outcomes rather than past costs\r\n  [S1].\r\n\r\n\u001b[1mCitations\u001b[0m (answered_with_citations)\r\n  [S1] raw/micro-examples-in-the-wild-1.md › Examples in the Wild 1\r\n  [S3] raw/micro-final-cheat-sheet.md › Costs\r\n  [S4] raw/micro-examples-in-the-wild-1.md › Examples in the Wild 1\r\n\r\n\u001b[2m7.01 s · 35.4 tok/s · MLX peak 5.85 GB · saved runs/ask/20260922-182159-what-is-a-sunk-cost-and-how-"]
[0.000, "o", "did-i-fall-into-the-su.md\u001b[0m"]
[0.000, "o", "\r\n"]
[0.148, "o", "\u001b[1m$ ./wiki ask What WACC did I use in my Kellanova DCF valuation?\u001b[0m\r\n"]
[9.483, "o", "\u001b[1mAsk\u001b[0m · mlx-community/gemma-4-e4b-it-4bit · local\r\n\r\n"]
