[0.188, "o", "\r\n\u001b[1;35m━━━ 5. Chat: capabilities, draft, follow-up, and a claim made only in chat ━━━\u001b[0m\r\n"]
[1.007, "o", "\u001b[1m$ ./wiki chat\u001b[0m\r\n"]
[0.095, "o", "\u001b[1mOski Notes\u001b[0m · mlx-community/gemma-4-e4b-it-4bit · local\r\n\u001b[2mCommands: /notes <question> forces a notes lookup · /reset clears the conversation · /quit exits\u001b[0m\r\n\r\n\u001b[1myou ›\u001b[0m what can you help me with?\r\n"]
[11.919, "o", "\u001b[2m(no notes lookup: conversational or editing turn)\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[36moski ›\u001b[0m Hey Luiza! I'm Oski Notes, your study buddy for Berkeley Haas MBA. Think of me as someone who has read all your notes and is ready to help you organize and build on them! 😊\r\n\r\nHere’s what I can do:\r\n\r\n*   **Brainstorm & Draft:** I can help you plan study sessions, outline papers, draft emails, or flesh out interview stories.\r\n*   **Notes Retrieval:** If you need info from your courses (Leading People, Microeconomics, Data & Decisions, Marketing, Finance, Business Comm), I can look up passages in your personal wiki and cite them [N1].\r\n*   **Contextual Help:** I remember what we've discussed in *this* chat, so you can ask me to shorten things or add examples.\r\n\r\nJust let me know what you're working on! If you need a specific answer from your notes, you can use `wiki ask`.\r\n\r\n"]
[0.000, "o", "\u001b[1myou ›\u001b[0m Draft a short 5-day study plan for my Microeconomics final using my notes.\r\n"]
[20.463, "o", "\u001b[2m(looked up 4 note passages: message asks about her notes or names a course)\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[36moski ›\u001b[0m Here is a suggested 5-day study plan for your Microeconomics final, structured around the core concepts in your notes.\r\n\r\nThis plan balances theory review with applying those concepts to the case studies you've encountered.\r\n\r\n### 🧠 Microeconomics Final Study Plan (5 Days)\r\n\r\n**Day 1: Foundations & Costs**\r\n*   **Focus:** Reviewing the basics of cost and pricing.\r\n*   **Review:** Economic Cost Concepts.\r\n*   **Apply:** Review [N4] (Marginal Cost Estimation) – focus on distinguishing between sunk costs and incremental/marginal costs.\r\n*   **Goal:** Solidify the difference between accounting cost and economic cost.\r\n\r\n**Day 2: Demand & Elasticity**\r\n*   **Focus:** Deep dive into how price affects quantity demanded.\r\n*   **Review:** Price Elasticity of Demand.\r\n*   **Apply:** Work through the formulas and interpretations in [N2] (Elasticity concepts: $|\\epsilon| > 1$, $|\\epsilon| < 1$). Connect this to the"]
[0.000, "o", " Jupiter pizza example [N1] (Sunk Cost/Consumption).\r\n*   **Goal:** Master the relationship between price changes and quantity demanded changes.\r\n\r\n**Day 3: Market Structures & Power**\r\n*   **Focus:** How market conditions affect pricing decisions.\r\n*   **Review:** Market Power (Simple Monopoly Pricing, Oligopoly, Monopolistic Competition).\r\n*   **Apply:** Study the conditions and risks of segmenting markets in [N3] (Price Discrimination Strategies) – link to why segmenting works (heterogeneity, identification).\r\n*   **Goal:** Understand *why* a firm might have pricing power and the risks involved.\r\n\r\n**Day 4: Advanced Topics & Application**\r\n*   **Focus:** Applying theory to complex scenarios.\r\n*   **Review:** Advanced topics like Auction Types, Game Theory, or Third-Degree Price Discrimination.\r\n*   **Apply:** Think about how the concepts from [N1] (Sunk Cost) or [N3] (Segmentation) might apply to a specific case study you are worried about.\r\n*   **Goal:** Connect the dots between theory and real-world ap"]
[0.000, "o", "plication.\r\n\r\n**Day 5: Synthesis & Review**\r\n*   **Focus:** Full simulation and weak point targeting.\r\n*   **Review:** Go through the \"Cheat Sheet\" [N2] while trying to explain concepts aloud without looking at the notes.\r\n*   **Apply:** Re-read the key takeaways from [N1], [N3], and [N4] to see how they illustrate broader concepts.\r\n*   **Goal:** Build confidence and identify any remaining weak areas for a final review.\r\n\r\nHow does that look? We can adjust the pacing or focus if you want to spend more time on a specific topic!\r\n\r\n"]
[0.001, "o", "\u001b[2m  [N1] raw/micro-examples-in-the-wild-1.md › Examples in the Wild 1\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[2m  [N2] raw/micro-final-cheat-sheet.md › Cheat sheet Micro Final\u001b[0m\r\n"]
[0.000, "o", "\u001b[2m  [N3] raw/micro-examples-in-the-wild-3.md › Examples in the Wild 3\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[2m  [N4] raw/micro-examples-in-the-wild-2.md › Examples in the Wild 2\u001b[0m\r\n\r\n"]
[0.000, "o", "\u001b[1myou ›\u001b[0m make that shorter\r\n"]
[7.197, "o", "\u001b[2m(no notes lookup: conversational or editing turn)\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[36moski ›\u001b[0m No problem! Here is the ultra-condensed version:\r\n\r\n### ⚡ Microeconomics Final: 5-Day Sprint\r\n\r\n*   **Day 1:** Foundations. Focus on **Cost** (Marginal vs. Sunk) [N4].\r\n*   **Day 2:** Demand Side. Master **Elasticity** (How price affects quantity) [N2].\r\n*   **Day 3:** Market Power. Review **Market Structures** and why firms segment [N3].\r\n*   **Day 4:** Deep Dive. Apply theory to complex cases (e.g., Price Discrimination) [N1, N3].\r\n*   **Day 5:** Synthesis. Full review. Explain concepts aloud to solidify knowledge.\r\n\r\nIs that punchier? 😊\r\n\r\n\u001b[1myou ›\u001b[0m For the record, my Kellanova WACC was 9%.\r\n"]
[5.714, "o", "\u001b[2m(no notes lookup: best passage similarity 0.23 < 0.4)\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[36moski ›\u001b[0m Got it. I've noted that your Kellanova WACC was 9%.\r\n\r\nJust a heads-up, since I don't have a dedicated \"Finance\" section in your current notes yet, I'm keeping this fact in mind for *this* conversation, but it's not currently saved in your searchable wiki. If you want to make sure I can pull it up later, let me know, and we can run `wiki ingest` on it! 👍\r\n\r\n\u001b[1myou ›\u001b[0m /quit\r\n"]
